<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maharashtra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maharashtra is a state of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, created in 1960 from the Marathi-speaking parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a> as its capital. Its territory includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konkan-coast">Konkan coast</a> and much of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan plateau</a>. The region was ruled in turn by the Satavahanas, Rashtrakutas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Yadavas of Devagiri</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-sultanates">Deccan sultanates</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, and it is the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> language.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maharashtra.jpg" alt="Maharashtra" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jawahar Soneji · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gateway_of_India_-Mumbai.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Maharashtra covers 307,713 square kilometres of the western peninsular region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and occupies a substantial part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a>. It has a coastline of 840 kilometres on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>. The dominant feature is the plateau, separated from the narrow Konkan coastal plains by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a>, also called the Sahyadri Range, which run parallel to the coast and average 1,200 metres in height. The Satpura Hills form its northern border. The Godavari and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> are its two major rivers. Its main regions are North Maharashtra, Konkan, Marathwada, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidarbha">Vidarbha</a> and Desh, or Western Maharashtra.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region has a history of many thousands of years. Late Harappan or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcolithic">Chalcolithic</a> sites of the Jorwe culture, c. 1300 to 700 BCE, have been found throughout the state, the largest at Daimabad, which had a mud fortification and an elliptical temple with fire pits. In the Late Harappan period many people migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> to northern Maharashtra. Among the dynasties that ruled the region were the Asmakas, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Mauryas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abhira-people">Abhiras</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakataka-dynasty">Vakatakas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a>, the Western Chalukyas, the Khaljis, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughlaqs</a>, the Bahamanis and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, besides the Satavahanas, Rashtrakutas and Seuna Yadavas.</p><p>In the early 19th century the region was divided between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a>'s dominions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-confederacy">Maratha Confederacy</a> and the Nizamate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>. The British East India Company then defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and brought most of present-day Maharashtra under its control, directly and through many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, and from 1858 the British crown took over. The region was divided among the Bombay Presidency, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-provinces">Central Provinces</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a> and princely states including Hyderabad. After independence in 1947 the Bombay Presidency became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a>, which gained Berar, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> states and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> states between 1950 and 1956. The United Maharashtra Movement campaigned for a separate state for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> speakers, and on 1 May 1960 Bombay State was divided into Maharashtra and Gujarat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a> is the capital of Maharashtra, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> as its winter capital, and the state has 36 districts in six divisions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a>, written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devanagari">Devanagari</a> script, is the official language and is spoken by about 69 per cent of the people. Its earliest literature is the Dnyaneshwari, a commentary on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a> by the 13th-century Bhakti saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnyaneshwar">Dnyaneshwar</a>. Seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a> lie in the state, among them the Ajanta, Ellora and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elephanta-caves">Elephanta Caves</a> and the Chhatrapati Shivaji Terminus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maharashtra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Satavahanas rose in the northern Deccan, in what is now <b>Maharashtra</b>, after the Maurya Empire withdrew from the region in the second century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Maharashtra</b> Dnyaneshwar completed the Dnyaneshwari, his Marathi commentary on the Bhagavad Gita, in 1290; Namdev and his companions sang of the god Vithoba of Pandharpur, the focus of the Varkari tradition, which Eknath carried on in the 16th century and Tukaram, who died about 1650, in the…</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>A more radical group, led by Bal Gangadhar Tilak in <b>Maharashtra</b>, Bipin Chandra Pal in Bengal and Lala Lajpat Rai in the Punjab, demanded swaraj, self-rule, and urged swadeshi, the use of Indian goods, together with the boycott of British ones and national education; Tilak’s declaration that swaraj…</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>As the Satavahana dynasty weakened in the early 3rd century, an Abhira chief, Ishvarasena, established a kingdom around Nashik in <b>Maharashtra</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/abhira-dynasty">Abhira dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Akkalkot merged into Bombay Province in 1948 and became part of <b>Maharashtra</b> in 1960, in Solapur district.</blockquote><a href="/state/akalkot-state">Akalkot State</a></li><li><blockquote>Aundh merged into Bombay Province in 1948 and became part of <b>Maharashtra</b> in 1960, in Satara district.</blockquote><a href="/state/aundh-state">Aundh State</a></li></ul></section>
